<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Headaches in bouts that come on in the morning and stop him reading or writing for days or weeks, now at the start of a demanding new post. [S]</item><item>He wants to know whether something serious is causing them, and whether anything works better than the remedies he has tried. [S]</item><item>Writing by hand is slow and painful because of an old wrist injury, and his work depends on writing. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Thomas Jefferson was born at Shadwell, in what was then Goochland County, Virginia, on April 13, 1743 (April 2, 1743, in the Old Style calendar then in use). [D]</item><item>At 20, in a letter of March 20, 1764, written at Williamsburg late at night, he wrote of &quot;a violent head ach, with which I have been afflicted these two days.&quot; [D]</item><item>In 1776 an illness kept him at home in Virginia for about six weeks longer than he intended; a correspondent wrote that his time at home had been interrupted by &quot;an inveterate head ach.&quot; [D]</item><item>His wife died on September 6, 1782, of complications after childbirth. In November 1782 he wrote that he was only &quot;a little emerging from that stupor of mind which had rendered me as dead to the world as she was whose loss occasioned it.&quot; [D]</item><item>In March 1784, at Annapolis as a delegate to Congress, he wrote that he had &quot;lately added an attack of my periodical headach&quot; to his habitual ill health and had to avoid reading, writing and almost thinking; his letters thereafter call the attacks his &quot;periodical head-ach.&quot; [D]</item><item>In September 1786, in Paris, he dislocated his right wrist in a fall; the bones were set, but not well. He wrote with his left hand for a time, and in December 1788 wrote that the joint &quot;had never been replaced,&quot; that he had lost the use of the hand &quot;except that I can write,&quot; and that &quot;a withered hand and swelled and crooked fingers&quot; remained. [D]</item><item>After five years in France as a diplomat, he took office as the first Secretary of State in New York City on March 21, 1790. [D]</item><item>In New York, a periodical headache &quot;came on me the 1st. of May&quot; 1790, and the first week was violent; his account books record purchases of Peruvian bark (cinchona) on May 1 and May 3. [D]</item></list><paragraph>12 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.20" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11348-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Past illness note"/>
    <title>Past Medical History</title>
    <text><paragraph>Historical biography with true dates. These events are not part of the synthetic care timeline.</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Historical date</th><th>History</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>from March 1764</td><td>Recurrent severe headaches in his letters from early adulthood, lasting from two days to several weeks, which he called his periodical headache</td><td>D (CLM-0002, CLM-0003, CLM-0004)</td></tr><tr><td>September 1782</td><td>Profound grief after his wife&apos;s death, which he later described as a stupor of mind; recorded without diagnosis</td><td>D (CLM-0005)</td></tr><tr><td>September 1786</td><td>Dislocation of the right wrist in a fall, set badly; lasting stiffness, swelling and crooked fingers, though he can write</td><td>D (CLM-0006)</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

