    <text><list><item>Diagnosis: a structured headache history, a red-flag screen (SNNOOP10) at every visit, a 14-day headache diary of attacks, features, triggers and medicine days, and ICHD-3 criteria; no neuroimaging for a stable long-standing pattern without red flags, with clear reasons that would change that. Guideline: Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society (IHS). The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Choosing wisely in headache medicine: the American Headache Society&apos;s list of five things physicians and patients should question (2013) [S]</item><item>Neurology (headache medicine) consultation to confirm the diagnosis and advise on acute and preventive treatment. Guideline: The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Calcitonin gene-related peptide-targeting therapies are a first-line option for the prevention of migraine: An American Headache Society position statement update (2024) [S]</item><item>Acute treatment at intake: naproxen early in an attack, with metoclopramide for nausea, no more than two days a week; stop the over-the-counter combination analgesic with caffeine; no opioids or butalbital. Guideline: The American Headache Society Position Statement On Integrating New Migraine Treatments Into Clinical Practice (2019); Pharmacologic Treatments of Acute Episodic Migraine Headache in Outpatient Settings: A Clinical Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2025); Choosing wisely in headache medicine: the American Headache Society&apos;s list of five things physicians and patients should question (2013) [S]</item><item>Medication overuse headache prevention: acute-medicine days counted in the diary, with limits of fewer than 10 days a month for a triptan or combination analgesic and fewer than 15 for an NSAID; never caffeine pills or combination products. Guideline: Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society (IHS). The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021) [S]</item><item>Lifestyle and triggers: a fixed wake and sleep time, regular meals on working days, a steady modest amount of caffeine, fluids, daily walks, and a stress plan for long working days with breaks from reading; relaxation training and biofeedback offered. Guideline: The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021) [S]</item><item>Wrist and writing: an occupational therapy visit for hand function, a wrist splint for long writing sessions, dictation software, and a desk set up to spare the wrist and neck. Guideline: The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021) [S]</item><item ID="referral-order">Referral to neurology (headache medicine), Farida Haddad, MD, ordered 2026-05-07 [S]</item></list></text>

